            <ArticleTitle>Prevalence of wheat allergy in Al-Kharj, Saudi Arabia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>52-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Wheat allergy has been suggested to represent an important allergic disease. This study collates clinical and laboratory aspects in patients with wheat allergy in Al-Kharj city, Saudi Arabia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Total and specific IgE were measured in 15 suspected cases of wheat allergy. Protein allergenicity was assessed with Western blotting.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Significant elevation of total and specific IgE was found in 4 cases. Basophlia was also demonstrated on blood film. Western blotting results showed 2 bands (83 and 40 kDa).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Wheat allergy must also be considered when planning treatment of asthma and eczema of adult patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
